\section{Separate Binding Policy from Its Index}

Assume a language with distinct value and type namespaces, lexical
scope, and sequential value declarations. In a declaration
\texttt{let x = x + 1}, the initializer sees the old environment;
only after the initializer is resolved does the newly declared
\texttt{x} become visible. Meanwhile a type called \texttt{x} may
coexist with either value. None of these rules is an automatic
consequence of using a hash table. A compiler must encode the key
as a namespace and symbol pair and define the points at which
declarations enter the environment. It is helpful to store each
declaration's source site and stable-for-the-resolution identity
in a separate declaration store. A scoped map then contains only
the identities of visible declarations.

Appel contrasts imperative installations followed by stacklike
restoration with functional environments that keep old mappings
available \cite{appel2004modern}. Both can implement the sequential
example. The choice is about time travel: do we only move forward
and temporarily descend into a child scope, or must several tasks
query different historical environments at once? In
\Cref{fig:visual-420}, two sibling bodies begin from the same
published environment. The `x' declarations made by them are distinct
even when both maps eventually contain the same key set.

