\section{Pay for the Shape of Lookup You Actually Need}

With one map per scope and a parent link, lookup can stop at the first
scope with a matching key. That makes duplicate detection simple:
inspect only the current map. The worst-case number of maps inspected
is the depth of the scope chain, notably for missing names. Small
sorted vectors may beat a hash table for scopes consisting of two
parameters and one local; they avoid a capacity-sized allocation and
keep keys next to each other. A hybrid can promote a scope once its
entries exceed a measured threshold. Ensure the change of
representation doesn't change lookup order for constructs such as
overload groups.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
Representation & Lookup path & Scope exit \\
\hline
Parent-linked maps & Test current, then ancestor maps & Restore parent pointer \\
Current map with undo log & Test one visible map & Undo local declarations \\
Persistent balanced map & Descend one tree & Drop current root \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

An undo log keeps a single map of the visible winners. It is an
especially good fit for a nonparallel depth-first resolver, since an
unknown name takes one ordinary map lookup and the log needs one
record per installed declaration. Snapshots of this implementation
are not cheap: saving the log length does not preserve an independent
map that a concurrent task can read. The log length is a cursor for
restoration, not a shareable map version.

% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Restore Shadowed Values in Exact Reverse Order}

\begin{verbatim}
begin_scope():
    return (fresh_scope_id(), undo.length)

declare(scope, space, symbol, decl):
    key := (space, symbol)
    previous := visible.get(key)    // Some(binding) or None
    if previous exists and previous.scope == scope.id:
        report_duplicate(decl, previous.declaration)
        return
    undo.push((key, previous))
    visible[key] := Binding(scope.id, decl)

resolve_sequential_let(scope, name, init):
    resolved_init := resolve_expression(init, visible)
    decl := new_declaration(name, resolved_init, scope.id)
    declare(scope, ValueSpace, name.symbol, decl)
    return decl

end_scope(scope):
    require this is the innermost open scope
    while undo.length > scope.undo_position:
        key, previous := undo.pop()
        if previous exists: visible[key] := previous
        else: visible.remove(key)
\end{verbatim}

Beginning a scope need not change visibility at all. A name's
previous value is saved immediately before updating the current map;
the record contains absence explicitly so restoration does not leave
a tombstone that shadows an ancestor. A duplicate in this example is
diagnosed without replacing the first declaration. That policy lets
later uses resolve consistently during recovery. A language with
mutually recursive functions needs a different installation sequence:
enter all headers, then check all bodies under the shared extended
environment. Moving a single-binding operation across the body
boundary would change which recursion patterns the language supports.

Run a trace with an outer value \texttt{x} and nested blocks declaring
\texttt{x}, then \texttt{y}, then \texttt{x} again in a deeper
block. Read \texttt{x} after each close and read \texttt{y} after
its declaring block closes. Include a type named \texttt{x}; the
value undo operation must not touch its entry. If an error return from
one block bypasses restoration, the next sibling inherits a
declaration that does not lexically enclose it. A scope guard or a
structured traversal that executes scope exit even on early returns
makes this failure harder to introduce.

\section{Publish Roots When Readers Need Snapshots}

A persistent balanced search tree path-copies nodes along an
insertion, including nodes changed by rebalancing. With \(n\)
keys this costs \(O(\log n)\) time and new nodes per insertion,
while an old root continues to name the old map. A hash-array mapped
trie trades ordering for branching by successive groups of hash
bits; it needs a specific representation for collisions. In either
case, a persistent flattened map can carry the declaring scope in
its values, so a lookup still distinguishes a duplicate from legal
shadowing. Persisting the map is not enough if its values are mutable
and their meaning changes after publication. Use immutable entries or
separately version any semantic facts intentionally updated later.

Do not equate environment root identity with language equivalence.
Two independently built roots may contain the same bindings; returning
to exactly the old root after a scope closes is a cheap sufficient
test for reuse, not a necessary one. Conversely a cached lookup for
\texttt{x} depends on what shadows it and what namespace was searched,
not just on the declaration it returned. Adding an inner declaration
can change a lookup even when none of the old declarations changes.
For a cache that tracks individual lookups, record the relevant
negative searches as dependencies. Otherwise keep environment root
identity in the cache key and accept coarser invalidation.

\section{Measure Lifetime, Not Just Lookup Latency}

Use three benchmark shapes: a single large module, deeply nested
small blocks with many misses, and many simultaneous environment
snapshots. Record successful and failed probes separately as well
as peak live nodes, not only allocations. A persistent environment
can free most copied paths promptly when readers drop their roots;
retaining every root in a diagnostic history makes the same algorithm
look much worse. Check identical resolver outputs across
representations using source sites of declarations, not allocation
order of declaration IDs. The exercise reveals which performance
differences are actually attributable to the map and which come
from keeping unnecessary history alive.

\section{Do Not Model Imports as Arbitrary Parent Links}

Consider a module that imports public value \texttt{paint} from
both \texttt{Colors} and \texttt{Canvas}, then declares no local
\texttt{paint}. A single lexical parent chain gives one of those
modules priority even if the language reports ambiguity. Use a
module export index and a separate import tier, with ordering and
visibility rules specified independently of the local map. For a
simple language, search for the key in the current lexical
chain first, then collect public imports
only if the lexical search returned no value binding; report
ambiguity if more than one distinct declaration remains. If two
imports reexport the same underlying declaration, deduplicating
by declaration identity rather than textual name makes the policy
explicit. A lexical namespace cannot implement linker resolution
by itself; compare the three boundaries in \Cref{ch:ext-397}.

Store candidate provenance with ambiguous results. A deterministic
diagnostic can sort by qualified module name and declaration site
independently of the map's probe order. That is useful even if
batch and editor resolvers use entirely different maps. Test a
module that adds a local value to resolve the ambiguity, and a
module that adds only a local type of the same name. In the latter
case the value lookup must still report the two imported candidates.
